Iterators / Iterables / Collections

-- Iterables allow you the ability to return an Iterator.

-- An Iterator has state and reflects the current state of traversal.

-- Collections HAVE Iterables. Other things can have Iterables too aka return you multiple Iterator objects

--------
Algorithms work on Iterators.

Now having cleared this up lets disect C++ STL and Java.

-- In Java Iterables are interfaces implemented by Collections and they return Iterator objects which can then be traversed.
   -- Now as it turns out, there is just one kind of an Iterator in Java aka Bidirectional Iterators

-- In C++ STL, Iterables are implicit. vectors, maps silently implement it which is why they have methods like begin(), end(),
   cbegin, cend(), rbegin(), rend() which allows them to return Iterators.

IMHO there really is no difference between these approaches. Except STL provides a lot many types of Iterators and Iterables.

